  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 346 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nQuiet weather continues. The Lower Ohio Valley remains under an \nupper ridge with a surface ridge of high pressure extending \nsouthwest into the area from Ohio. Winds remain light out of the \neast as temperatures are either side of 70 across southern Indiana \nand central Kentucky.\n\nTonight, the surface high gets pushed farther to the northeast, near \ncalm surface winds will respond by veering towards the southeast \nunder mostly clear skies to start the night. Later in the night, \nhigh level clouds will build in from the southeast, mainly over the \nLake Cumberland and Bluegrass regions. Temperatures are expected to \ndrop into the mid 30s to low 40s.\n\nSaturday and Sunday, ahead of an approaching north to south oriented \ncold front (extending south from a surface low moving east across \nOntario and the northern Great Lakes), light winds remain out of the \nsouth. Temperatures remain seasonally warm in the upper 60s to low \n70s. Winds ahead of the front never increase to more than around 5-\n10 mph.\n\nSunday evening into the overnight, the front arrives and moves east \nthrough the CWA. Winds quickly veer towards the northwest behind the \nfront, ushering in CAA. A line of rain along the front is expected to \nweaken as it moves east. No one is expected to receive too much rain \nfrom the system, but higher amounts are expected west of Interstate \n65 with lower amounts to the east. Some areas may only see around a \nhundredth or two with this system up to around a tenth of an inch. An \nisolated place or two could see higher amounts, but we are still \ntalking tenths of an inch and not inches. Severe weather isn't \nexpected, but winds are expected to gust to around 15 mph with the \npassage of the front.\n\nBehind the front, a surface high, centered over the northern Plains, \nwill keep CAA in place. Temperatures on Monday and Tuesday will be \nheld to mostly the low to mid 50s for highs and either side of \nfreezing for lows. \n\nGetting into the middle of the week, uncertainty increases as the \nsurface high passes east of the Ohio Valley and increased southern \nflow brings additional rain chances to the region. This trend is \nwidespread in the guidance, but as an upper trough moves east \ntowards the CWA, some models have an upper low strengthening north \nof the region and some have the low developing south of the region. \nBoth could result in precipitation, but a stronger southern low has \nthe potential for higher amounts of rainfall.",
